# Everything the installation medium installs, and nothing else.
#
# The list is deliberately not the smoke image's list. That one carries a
# whole desktop - AGS with the bar and the dock, five Hyprland plugins,
# zepos-menu,
# cliphist -
# because the question it answers is whether a ZepOS session comes up.
# This image answers a different question: can a person install ZepOS. So
# the desktop is not here; it is what gets INSTALLED, out of
# /opt/zepos-repo and the Arch mirrors, onto the target.
#
# Every package below is either something the installer calls, something
# the minimal Wayland session of spec §8.5 needs, or something without
# which the medium does not boot.

# --- the installer, which is what this medium is -----------------------
# Spec §4.2, one line under the table: "Die drei Installer-Pakete liegen
# nur in der ISO, nicht im installierten System."
#
# All three. §8.5's fallback from the GTK4 surface to the text one is a
# decision taken at run time on the machine in front of the user, and an
# image carrying only one surface has made that decision at build time.
# zepos-installer-gui is the only one of the three that names a toolkit;
# zepos-installer-tui depends on the core and on nothing else at all.
#
# zepos-installer also brings what actually does the work: archinstall
# (spec §8.1 - "Ein eigener Partitionierer waere Code, dessen Fehler
# fremde Festplatten loeschen"), and with it arch-install-scripts,
# dosfstools, e2fsprogs and the rest of the filesystem tooling; plus
# util-linux for the disk list, openssl for the password hashes and iwd
# for the wireless step.
zepos-installer
zepos-installer-gui
zepos-installer-tui

# The public half of the ZepOS signing key, and the only ZepOS package
# with an install scriptlet. Spec §8.6: signatures from the first ISO.
# Without it `pacman-key --populate` on the live medium has no ZepOS
# keyring to populate, and every package installed out of
# /opt/zepos-repo is refused for a key the machine was never told to
# trust - see /usr/local/bin/zepos-live-prepare.
zepos-keyring

# --- the minimal Wayland session (spec §8.5) ---------------------------
# WHY cage AND NOT zepos-hyprland, WHICH IS ZepOS'S OWN COMPOSITOR
#     The installer must run as root: archinstall says so and exits 1
#     otherwise, this medium has no sudo, and the alternative - a live
#     user with passwordless root - is a credential in an image that is
#     meant to carry none. Hyprland refuses to start as root. Measured
#     against the shipped binary of zepos-hyprland 0.56.1:
#
#         Hyprland was launched with superuser privileges, but the
#         privileges check is not omitted.
#         Hint: Use the --i-am-really-stupid flag to omit that check.
#
#     A shipping medium whose desktop session is started with a flag
#     called --i-am-really-stupid is not a shipping medium. cage is a
#     kiosk compositor - 27 KiB, no configuration file, every window
#     fullscreen, exits when its child does - which is exactly the
#     "minimale Wayland-Sitzung" §8.5 asks for and nothing more.
#
#     ZepOS's own compositor is what gets INSTALLED. It has no reason to
#     be the thing that installs it.
#
# mesa carries the software rasteriser. A machine whose GPU has no
# driver - the exact machine §8.5's fallback exists for - still gets a
# KMS device with dumb buffers, and Mesa's kms_swrast is the only path
# from wlroots' GBM/EGL requirement to a picture. When even that fails,
# zepos-live-session falls back to the text interface on the console.
#
# foot is the terminal the session starts the installer in. It is there
# so that §8.5's fallback is VISIBLE: if GTK4 cannot open a window,
# /usr/bin/zepos-install prints why and starts the text interface, and
# without a terminal around it that would happen on a screen showing
# nothing. foot rather than kitty because kitty needs OpenGL 3.3 and
# this is precisely the machine that may not have it - foot draws with
# the CPU.
cage
mesa
foot

# --- archiso baseline: without these the image does not boot -----------
base
linux
mkinitcpio
mkinitcpio-archiso

# syslinux is needed IN THE IMAGE, not only in the build container:
# archiso's bios.syslinux boot mode copies the loader out of the built
# root's /usr/lib/syslinux/bios/.
syslinux

# linux-firmware, which the smoke image deliberately leaves out.
#
# That was right for a virtio guest and is wrong here: this image is
# booted on somebody's laptop, and the wireless adapter that needs a
# firmware blob is the same adapter spec §8.4 hangs the offline
# fallback on. An installation medium that cannot bring up the machine's
# network is a medium that can only ever install offline.
linux-firmware

# --- fonts, or the installer is a row of boxes -------------------------
# Roboto first, because the installer's own stylesheet asks for it by
# name and fontconfig answers a missing family by substituting one it
# has - so a medium without it would show a perfectly working installer
# in somebody else's typeface, and nothing would say so. Fira Code is
# what the disk sizes and the log view are set in.
#
# cantarell is what libadwaita's default style asks for, dejavu is what
# foot resolves `monospace` to, and noto covers everything neither of
# them has.
ttf-roboto
ttf-fira-code
cantarell-fonts
ttf-dejavu
noto-fonts

# --- icons the GTK4 surface draws --------------------------------------
# GTK looks its named icons up in an icon theme; without one the buttons
# in the installer are empty rectangles.
adwaita-icon-theme
hicolor-icon-theme
